 The model uses the following paper to generate its output: Stede, Manfred, Maite Taboada, and Debopam Das. 'Annotation guidelines for rhetorical structure.' Manuscript. University of Potsdam and Simon Fraser University (2017).
EDUs:
[
  [1, "Cross-site scripting (XSS) is a specific type of injection attack found in web applications."],
  [2, "In XSS, the site allows an attacker a possibility of injecting client-side scripts into the application,"],
  [3, "which are then executed on other users' computers"],
  [4, "as they access the application."],
  [5, "The starting point of an XSS attack is injecting code to the application."],
  [6, "This can be done through, for example, a form or an API endpoint."],
  [7, "Unlike SQL injection, XSS does not lead to the code being executed on the server,"],
  [8, "instead, it is simply stored."],
  [9, "This results in the server compromising its users."],
  [10, "Whenever a user requests content from the server that has the malicious code,"],
  [11, "the server responds to the request normally,"],
  [12, "by sending the content to the user."],
  [13, "Now, when the user retrieves the content with the malicious code,"],
  [14, "the content will be processed by the browser"],
  [15, "and the code will be executed."],
  [16, "This can, for example, lead to the browser crashing or to worse effects such as compromising content stored within the browser."]
]

